  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 231 PM EDT Sat Sep 12 2026\n\nNow-Sunday...Compared to this time yesterday, showers and storms are \ntaking a little longer to develop. A fair amount of low/mid cloud \ncover and slower inland progression of the east coast breeze means \ntwo things: a slightly later start to storms and an afternoon sea \nbreeze collision nearer to or just east of I-4. The 15z XMR sounding \nindicates anomalously high PW values (2.31\") as well, similar to \nwhat was observed yesterday. Models are in fair agreement with the \neast coast breeze slowly making a trek westward this afternoon, \neventually colliding with the west coast breeze near the Orlando \nmetro after 4 PM. Any storms prior to sunset will be capable of \ntorrential rainfall, frequent lightning, and occasionally gusty \nwinds (40-50 mph). Light background flow and erratic storm motions \nmay allow for instances of minor flooding (ponding on roads) to \ndevelop. When thunder roars, go indoors! Rain and storms will be on \na quick downward trend after 8-9 PM with the loss of daytime heating.\n\nSunday looks to bring a lot of the same in terms of rain and storm \ncoverage. Mid level troughing over the northeast (well north of us) \nslides eastward while the H5 ridge axis stays in place locally. \nHighs in the upper 80s to low 90s will feel more like the low 100s \n(heat index) as early as lunchtime on Sunday. Even with increasing \nclouds through the day, it will feel very muggy. A more active west-\nto-east push of showers and storms is possible, based on some newer \nruns of CAM guidance. Like today, it could take until midday or \nearly afternoon until the east coast breeze starts to meander inland \n(accompanied by scattered rain/storms). Another sea breeze/outflow \ncollision closer to the Orlando metro seems plausible as convection \npeaks after 3-5 PM, dissipating around sunset.\n\nAt the beaches, a moderate risk of rip currents continues through \nthe rest of the weekend. \n\nMonday-Friday (modified)...An upper level trough and attendant \nsurface front slide east over the Atlantic Monday into Tuesday as \nhigh pressure builds over the Great Lakes/Northeast. The influence \nof the high pressure center expands Tuesday and Wednesday, resulting \nin a tighter pressure gradient along its southern flank. This alone \nwill increase onshore (ENE) winds locally by the middle of the week. \nLatest ensemble probabilities for 30+ mph gusts indicate a gusty day \nalong the immediate coast Tuesday, followed by gustier winds \nspilling inland on Wednesday (EPS 40-50%+ and NBM 60-80%+ prob. of \n30+ mph gusts). \n\nBeyond mid week, greater uncertainty exists within the synoptic \npattern. As an H5 trough continues to move offshore, a broadened \narea of low pressure rounds the eastern side of the mid- level \nridge anchored over the southeast U.S. A number of deterministic \nmodels show this area becoming a closed low while ensemble \nguidance maintains more of a broad open wave over the southwest \nAtlantic. The area of low pressure continues to round the decaying\nridge over the southeast U.S. late in the week, passing over \nFlorida and into the Gulf Thursday into Friday. Deep moisture \ngenerally persists through the extended forecast, continuing a \nperiod of near to above seasonal rain chances. Slightly above \nnormal temperatures are forecast Monday and Tuesday as highs reach\nthe low 90s (mid 90s in a few spots Monday). Increasing onshore \nflow should then help bring nearer-to-normal temperatures, with \nhighs ranging the upper 80s by Wednesday.",
